e\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/3f8a701b/img_008_35c4722a.png\" alt=\"five ninths\"></span>, statement II may or may not be true.<p>Choice A is incorrect. For the given equation to have no solution, both <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/3f8a701b/img_009_221a0602.png\" alt=\"a, equals 9 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/3f8a701b/img_010_dca28200.png\" alt=\"b is not equal to five ninths \"></span> must be true. Choice B is incorrect because it must also be true that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/3f8a701b/img_011_dca28200.png\" alt=\"b is not equal to five ninths \"></span>. Choice C is incorrect because when <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/3f8a701b/img_012_221a0602.png\" alt=\"a, equals 9 \"></span>, there are many values of <span class=\"italic\">b </span>that lead to an equation having no solution. That is, <span class=\"italic\">b</span> might be 5, but <span class=\"italic\">b</span> isn&rsquo;t required to be 5.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":13,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959638,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"3f8a701b","skill_cd":"H.A.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","createDate":1691007959638,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"10864-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"d443ef51-d0b2-4400-97aa-26776aeb1637","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"10864-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">The equation <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"9 x plus 5, equals a, times, open parenthesis, x plus b, close parenthesis\"></span></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">a</span> and <span class=\"italic\">b</span> are constants, has no solutions. Which of the following must be true?<p class=\"list_paragraph \">\n            <span class=\"math_expression \">I. <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, equals 9\"></span></span>\n          </p><p class=\"list_paragraph \">\n            <span class=\"math_expression \">II. <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b equals 5\"></span></span>\n          </p><p class=\"list_paragraph \">\n            <span class=\"math_expression \">III. <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b is not equal to five ninths\"></span></span>\n          </p></p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">None</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">I only</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">I and II only</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">I and III only</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. For a linear equation in a form <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, x plus b, equals, c x plus d\"></span> to have no solutions, the <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-terms must have equal coefficients and the remaining terms must not be equal. Expanding the right-hand side of the given equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"9 x plus 5, equals, a, x plus a, b\"></span>. Inspecting the <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-terms, 9 must equal <span class=\"italic\">a, </span>so statement I must be true. Inspecting the remaining terms, 5 can&rsquo;t equal <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"9 b\"></span><span class=\"italic\">. </span>Dividing both of these quantities by 9 yields that <span class=\"italic\">b</span> can&rsquo;t equal <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"five ninths\"></span>. Therefore, statement III must be true. Since <span class=\"italic\">b</span> can have any value other than <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"five ninths\"></span>, statement II may or may not be true.<p>Choice A is incorrect. For the given equation to have no solution, both <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, equals 9 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b is not equal to five ninths \"></span> must be true. Choice B is incorrect because it must also be true that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b is not equal to five ninths \"></span>. Choice C is incorrect because when <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, equals 9 \"></span>, there are many values of <span class=\"italic\">b </span>that lead to an equation having no solution. That is, <span class=\"italic\">b</span> might be 5, but <span class=\"italic\">b</span> isn&rsquo;t required to be 5.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959638,"updateDate":1691007959638}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959638, 1691007959638),
    ($SATQ$40049d49$SATQ$, $SATQ$162bcb97-414c-46ed-a490-4d88402f07fa$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear equations in one variable$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In the given equation, <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"08de912a-85c1-49fc-939a-a439b3699a92","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c3566d13-00dc-4879-855c-9254de548d2d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c2ceb17d-8edc-4eab-a655-1e488c5f32e3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. If an equation has infinitely many solutions, then the two sides of the equation must be equivalent. Multiplying each side of the given equation by <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> yields&nbsp;<math alttext="8 x plus 24 equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>8</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>24</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mi>a</mi>
		<mfenced>
			<mrow>
				<mi>x</mi>
				<mo>+</mo>
				<mn>3</mn>
			</mrow>
		</mfenced>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Since <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math> is a common factor of both terms on the left-hand side of this equation, the equation can be rewritten as <math alttext="8 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis"><mn>8</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. The two sides of this equation are equivalent when <math alttext="a equals 8"><mrow>
	<mi>a</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>8</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, if the given equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Alternate approach: If the given equation, <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, has infinitely many solutions, then both sides of this equation are equal for any value of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>. If <math alttext="x equals 0"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>0</mn>
</mrow>
</math>, then substituting <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> in the given equation yields <math alttext="4 left parenthesis 0 right parenthesis plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis 0 plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="12 equals three halves a"><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>a</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="three halves"><mfrac>
	<mn>3</mn>
	<mn>2</mn>
</mfrac>
</math> yields <math alttext="8 equals a"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals 0"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>12</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>0</mn>
</mrow>
</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 3 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals three halves x plus nine halves"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="12"><mn>12</mn>
</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 12 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="4 x plus 12 equals 6 x plus 18"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>12</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>6</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>18</mn>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"40049d49","external_id":"162bcb97-414c-46ed-a490-4d88402f07fa","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"d10a2929-a000-45ad-8f9d-c253e24a95b8","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.A.","skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the given equation, <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"08de912a-85c1-49fc-939a-a439b3699a92","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c3566d13-00dc-4879-855c-9254de548d2d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c2ceb17d-8edc-4eab-a655-1e488c5f32e3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. If an equation has infinitely many solutions, then the two sides of the equation must be equivalent. Multiplying each side of the given equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields&nbsp;<math alttext=\"8 x plus 24 equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>8</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>24</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t\t<mfenced>\n\t\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t</mfenced>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Since <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> is a common factor of both terms on the left-hand side of this equation, the equation can be rewritten as <math alttext=\"8 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>8</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. The two sides of this equation are equivalent when <math alttext=\"a equals 8\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>8</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, if the given equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Alternate approach: If the given equation, <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, has infinitely many solutions, then both sides of this equation are equal for any value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>. If <math alttext=\"x equals 0\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>0</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, then substituting <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> in the given equation yields <math alttext=\"4 left parenthesis 0 right parenthesis plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis 0 plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"12 equals three halves a\"><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>a</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"three halves\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"8 equals a\"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals 0\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>0</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 3 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals three halves x plus nine halves\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 12 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals 6 x plus 18\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>6</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357387,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"40049d49","skill_cd":"H.A.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","createDate":1730147357387,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"","external_id":"162bcb97-414c-46ed-a490-4d88402f07fa","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"d10a2929-a000-45ad-8f9d-c253e24a95b8","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>","id":"08de912a-85c1-49fc-939a-a439b3699a92"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math></p>","id":"c3566d13-00dc-4879-855c-9254de548d2d"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>","id":"270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math></p>","id":"c2ceb17d-8edc-4eab-a655-1e488c5f32e3"}],"externalid":"162bcb97-414c-46ed-a490-4d88402f07fa","keys":["270edc3b-a518-4361-bdcf-6bac368a7e7b"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. If an equation has infinitely many solutions, then the two sides of the equation must be equivalent. Multiplying each side of the given equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields&nbsp;<math alttext=\"8 x plus 24 equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>8</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>24</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t\t<mfenced>\n\t\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t</mfenced>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Since <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> is a common factor of both terms on the left-hand side of this equation, the equation can be rewritten as <math alttext=\"8 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis equals a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>8</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. The two sides of this equation are equivalent when <math alttext=\"a equals 8\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>8</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, if the given equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Alternate approach: If the given equation, <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, has infinitely many solutions, then both sides of this equation are equal for any value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>. If <math alttext=\"x equals 0\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>0</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, then substituting <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> in the given equation yields <math alttext=\"4 left parenthesis 0 right parenthesis plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis 0 plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"12 equals three halves a\"><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>a</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"three halves\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"8 equals a\"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals 0\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>0</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 3 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals three halves x plus nine halves\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. If the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math>, the given equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction 12 left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals 6 x plus 18\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>6</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>, which has one solution, not infinitely many solutions.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4 x plus 12 equals StartFraction a left parenthesis x plus 3 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the given equation, <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>?</p>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1730147357387,"updateDate":1730147357387}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357387, 1730147357387),
    ($SATQ$400798d6$SATQ$, $SATQ$df2106f5-60f0-4169-a44d-b3223cb59788$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="2 x plus y equals 37"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>2</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mi>y</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>37</mn>
</mrow>
</math></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In triangle <math alttext="upper Q upper R upper S"><mrow>
	<mi>Q</mi>
	<mi>R</mi>
	<mi>S</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mrow>
	<mi>Q</mi>
	<mi>R</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper R upper S"><mrow>
	<mi>R</mi>
	<mi>S</mi>
</mrow>
</math> each have a length of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> centimeters and side&nbsp;<math alttext="upper S upper Q"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> has a length of <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> centimeters. The given equation represents this situation. Which of the following is the best interpretation of <math alttext="37"><mn>37</mn>
</math> in this context?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b07ab78a-5d10-4cca-9ed7-726b6d8c167e","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper S upper Q\"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"3bb70e53-5133-49e6-b70a-cb2e58f0dcd1","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"253e2c8f-3162-4558-92f1-db200ac276a2","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of the three sides of the triangle</p>"},{"id":"bf1d2064-7b38-4be4-b5cb-2e715921feb1","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The length, in centimeters, of one of the two sides of equal length</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["253e2c8f-3162-4558-92f1-db200ac276a2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. It's given that in triangle <math alttext="upper Q upper R upper S"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>, sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext="upper R upper S"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> each have a length of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> centimeters. Therefore, the expression <math alttext="2 x"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math> represents the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext="upper R upper S"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>. It's also given that side <math alttext="upper S upper Q"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> has a length of <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> centimeters. Therefore, the expression <math alttext="2 x plus y"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></math> represents the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, <math alttext="upper R upper S"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>, and <math alttext="upper S upper Q"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math>. Since <math alttext="2 x plus y"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></math> is the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of the three sides of the triangle and <math alttext="2 x plus y equals 37"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></math>, it follows that <math alttext="37"><mn>37</mn>
</math> is the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of the three sides of the triangle.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext="upper S upper Q"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math alttext="x minus y"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, not <math alttext="37"><mn>37</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext="upper Q upper R"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext="upper R upper S"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is <math alttext="x minus x"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math>, not <math alttext="37"><mn>37</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. The length, in centimeters, of one of the two sides of equal length is <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>, not <math alttext="37"><mn>37</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"400798d6","external_id":"df2106f5-60f0-4169-a44d-b3223cb59788","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"e7bfb4da-3480-422c-935a-6963676a7b1f","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.C.","skill_desc":"Linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2 x plus y equals 37\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>37</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R upper S\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> each have a length of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> centimeters and side&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper S upper Q\"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> has a length of <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> centimeters. The given equation represents this situation. Which of the following is the best interpretation of <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math> in this context?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b07ab78a-5d10-4cca-9ed7-726b6d8c167e","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper S upper Q\"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"3bb70e53-5133-49e6-b70a-cb2e58f0dcd1","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<