 x plus y\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></math> is the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of the three sides of the triangle and <math alttext=\"2 x plus y equals 37\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></math>, it follows that <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math> is the sum of the lengths, in centimeters, of the three sides of the triangle.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"upper S upper Q\"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"x minus y\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, not <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. The difference, in centimeters, between the lengths of sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"x minus x\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, not <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. The length, in centimeters, of one of the two sides of equal length is <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>, not <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math>.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2 x plus y equals 37\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>37</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R upper S\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, sides <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> each have a length of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> centimeters and side&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper S upper Q\"><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> has a length of <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> centimeters. The given equation represents this situation. Which of the following is the best interpretation of <math alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn>\n</math> in this context?</p>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1730147357391,"updateDate":1730147357391}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357391, 1730147357391),
    ($SATQ$4047cfa7$SATQ$, $SATQ$782fda69-e7ad-4084-b602-feec64776b52$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear inequalities in one or two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">For a literature class, a student's essay needs to have no more than <math alttext="5,000"><mn>5,000</mn>
</math> words. If a student's essay already has <math alttext="3,026"><mn>3,026</mn>
</math> words, what is the maximum number of additional words the student can include in the essay?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6499b7be-b7f0-4c03-9444-18696db1f0f3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1,974\"><mn>1,974</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1f9ba784-1ff9-4bef-a71f-4cfa42dfcad2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3,026\"><mn>3,026</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dd926ced-94a3-4e58-aa0b-73f3e54fc36b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"5,000\"><mn>5,000</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"78d306aa-a163-4d4d-9e3c-96736ca2ddb4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8,026\"><mn>8,026</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6499b7be-b7f0-4c03-9444-18696db1f0f3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. It's given that a student's essay needs to have no more than&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5,000"><mn>5,000</mn></math> words. It&rsquo;s also given that the student&rsquo;s essay already has <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3,026"><mn>3,026</mn></math> words. Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the number of additional words the student includes in the essay. After including <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> additional words, the essay will have a total of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3,026 plus x"><mn>3,026</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></math> words. Since the essay needs to have no more than <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5,000"><mn>5,000</mn></math> words, it follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3,026 plus x less than or equals 5,000"><mn>3,026</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>5,000</mn></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3,026"><mn>3,026</mn></math> from both sides of this inequality yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x less than or equals 1,974"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>1,974</mn></math>. Thus, the maximum number of additional words the student can include in the essay is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1,974"><mn>1,974</mn></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the number of words already in the student's essay, rather than the maximum number of additional words the student can include.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the total number of words the essay can contain, rather than the maximum number of additional words the student can include.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of words the essay would contain if the student added <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5,000"><mn>5,000</mn></math> more words, rather than the maximum number of additional words the student can include.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4047cfa7","external_id":"782fda69-e7ad-4084-b602-feec64776b52","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"4fa166b8-b140-4c58-8f5a-096c5e8c40e9","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.E.","skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For a literature class, a student's essay needs to have no more than <math alttext=\"5,000\"><mn>5,000</mn>\n</math> words. If a student's essay already has <math alttext=\"3,026\"><mn>3,026</mn>\n</math> words, what is the maximum number of additional words the student can include in the essay?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6499b7be-b7f0-4c03-9444-18696db1f0f3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1,974\"><mn>1,974</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1f9ba784-1ff9-4bef-a71f-4cfa42dfcad2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3,026\"><mn>3,026</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dd926ced-94a3-4e58-aa0b-73f3e54fc36b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"5,000\"><mn>5,000</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"78d306aa-a163-4d4d-9e3c-96736ca2ddb4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8,026\"><mn>8,026</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["6499b7be-b7f0-4c03-9444-18696db1f0f3"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. It's given that a student's essay needs to have no more than&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5,000\"><mn>5,000</mn></math> words. It&rsquo;s also given that the student&rsquo;s essay already has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w